X.if '\*(Bd'' .ds Bd moria
X.TH MORIA 6 "" "Local"
X.SH NAME
Xmoria \- a dungeon game
X.SH SYNOPSIS
X.B \*(Bd
X[
X.ie '\*O'ROGUE_LIKE' .B \-o
X.el .B \-r
X] [
X.B \-s
X] [ savefile ]
X.SH DESCRIPTION
X.I Moria\^
Xplays a dungeon game with you.
XIt generates a character for you,
Xlets you buy equipment,
Xand lets you wander in a fathomless dungeon
Xwhile finding treasure and being attacked by monsters
Xand fellow adventurers.
XTyping
X.B ?
Xgives you a list of commands.
X.PP
XThe ultimate object of
X.I moria
Xis to kill the Balrog,
Xwhich dwells on the 50th level of the
Xdungeon, 2,500 feet underground.
XMost players never even reach the Balrog,
Xand those that do seldom live to tell about it.
X.PP
XYou move in various directions
X.ie '\*O'ROGUE_LIKE' the same way you do in \fIrogue\fP(6).
X.el by pressing the numeric keypad keys, VMS-style.
XIf you specify
X.ie '\*O'ROGUE_LIKE' \fB\-o\fP, you move by pressing the numeric keypad keys, VMS-style.
X.el \fB\-r\fP, you move the same way you do in \fIrogue\fP(6).
X.PP
XIf you specify
X.BR \-s ,
X.I moria
Xprints a list of the twenty top scorers and exits.
X.SH AUTHORS
XThe original version of Moria was written for VMS by Robert
XAlan Koeneke, Jimmey Wayne Todd,
XGary McAdoo, and others at the University of Oklahoma.
XThis Unix version was written by Jim Wilson (ucbernie\^!\^wilson).
X.SH BUGS
X.PP
XThe SYS V version does not lock the scoreboard, if two processes try
Xto modify it at the same time, it may become corrupted.
X
XWearing an amulet or ring that would raise a stat over 18/100 actually only
Xraises the stat to 18/100.  When the amulet is removed, the stat drops to
Xa value below its original value.
X
XProbably infinite (although countably infinite).
END_OF_FILE
